\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\title{Connector Frequency in a Learner Corpus of Academic English}
\author{L. Hoshino and M. Okafor}
\date{July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Data}
The learner subcorpus contains 212{,}408 tokens of argumentative essays
written by advanced learners of English, and the reference subcorpus
contains 198{,}775 tokens of published journal prose.
All concordance lines were extracted with a case-insensitive query
and then deduplicated against the reference index.

\section{Frequency Profile}
Table~\ref{tab:freq} reports the raw counts for the three most
frequent sentence-initial connectors in each subcorpus.
\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lrr}
\hline
Connector & Learner & Reference \\
\hline
however & 1{,}204 & 986 \\
therefore & 873 & 1{,}051 \\
in addition & 1{,}672 & 402 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Raw counts of sentence-initial connectors per subcorpus.}
\label{tab:freq}

\section{Normalisation}
Raw counts were normalised per 100{,}000 tokens before comparison,
because the two subcorpora differ in size.
The learner essays overuse additive connectors by a wide margin,
while contrastive connectors are distributed more evenly.

\section{Discussion}
The overuse pattern is stable across proficiency bands
and does not correlate with essay score.
We interpret this as a teaching-induced inventory effect
rather than a developmental stage.

\end{document}

